Oh, yeah, I know the idea has come up lots of times,
has been around ever since Rog-O-Matic, and been
rejected because in the case of NetHack it is
stinking hard to do. That's why I proposed a patch
that could accurately append "(while stupid)" as a
half-way point. It is a classical ploy, when
programming, to break a big intractible problem down
into smaller and smaller intractible problems, until
the tractible level is reached, then code back up
until the whole problem is solved.

By the way, following the thread in which your link
is embedded leads to a Borg ascension, eventually,
and prompts a YANI, of course.

YANI: Borg Mode.

As an additional alternative to eXplore mode,
NetHack could have a Borg character class, or
alternately and probably better a play mode
"Borg-Mode" for all PC types, with special attack
spell "assimilate" that acted like taming, but shot
out like a breath weapon early on, or later like a
cone of cold.

Monsters once assimilated would _always_ remain
tame, and would _always_ follow the Borg from level
to level whether adjacent or not, whether busy or
not, whether stoned/frozen/dead or not.

The dungeon would be demolished by
groups/herds/flocks/swarms, yeah, swarms, of
assimilated living monsters, and still assimilate
undead monsters, chewing the opposition to shreds,
and assimilated stoned monsters falling on opponent
monsters' heads during level changes.

Visualize a Borg-mode player with dozens of
statuesque followers, running up and down stairs
doing the Monster Mash. Lots of fun on the quest.

Assimilated monsters that were killed would drop
corpses with only the current likelihood, giving
Borg-mode PCs at least some way to shed adherents.

Probably Borg ascensions shouldn't go in the high
score table either, just in the logfile. An extra
score component at death or ascension time would be
"monsters assimilated", a table like the "monsters
destroyed" table now. This would, of course, break
save and bones compatibility.

Playing in Borg mode would be a way for we
long-frustrated NetHackers to vent our fury on the
RNG. One Borg-mode game should be good enough of a
mental health boost to compensate for dozens and
dozens of YASDs.

